<p><FONT face="verdana, tahoma" size="2" >my thoughts on the subject as i drift to other areas...<br>
<br>
i was raised christian methodist, my father's side of the family being very religious. my mother never went to church. anyway, i sort of always accepted the dogma of my church, until one day, about 4 years ago, i began to "doubt". i was at a bible study run by a guy a couple years older than me, and he was telling us that we should basically give people of other religions a hard time because they were <i> wrong </i> and we were <i> right </i>. i asked how we knew we were right and they (everyone else) was wrong. the guy <i> yelled </i> at me. how could i ask such a question?? how could i doubt the divine judgement of god, blah blah blah... basically, i should go out and tell everyone who is not a christian that they are wrong and going to hell, huh? i didnt buy it. this experience opened my mind. i began to notice that the christian "rant" was the same from person to person, as if they all memorized it and were just spouting it. i suppose i lost my faith. i dont know. i never went back to that group, and i havent been to church since either.<br>
<br>
there has been an outbreak of christian dogma at my college recently. a religious group has been posting signs everywhere that read "do you agree with matt?", matt being matthew from the bible. i look around at these people and i find it hard to believe that they think they are so much better than other religions to be pushing christianity on everyone else. today i saw a sign that said "i agree with hume. religion is the opiate of the people". <br>
<br>
another thing i dont like about christianity is that no one focuses on the whole forgiveness/tolerance idea. its all about what is wrong. homosexuality is wrong, fornication is wrong. yadda yadda yadda. <br>
<br>
i still believe there is a God, but i dont yet know what to call him. i think that the christian god is the same as the jewish god is the same as the buddist god, etc, that its all the same thing, just different ways of worship. but then i was told i was a bad christian for thinking that way. <br>
<br>
i didnt mean for this to become such a long, bitter rant. my apologies, but this is a subject that im rather emotional about.<br>
<br>
kyt "the confused"<br>
